2 Tail bounds






LemTheorem 3 (Markov’s inequality)






Theorem 3 (Markov’s inequality) Let \(X\) be a random variable with \(X \ge 0\) and \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} &lt; \infty\). Then for every \(a &gt; 0\) [4]:
\[
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left(X \ge a\right)\mathclose{}} \le \frac{{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}}}{a}
\]










ProofProof






Proof. Because \(X \ge 0\), every outcome satisfies \(X \ge a \cdot \mathbb{1}_{{\mathopen{}\left\{X \ge a\right\}\mathclose{}}}\): when \(X \ge a\) the right side is \(a \le X\), and otherwise it is \(0 \le X\). The expectation of a nonnegative random variable is nonnegative, so by linearity of expectation, and because the expectation of an indicator is the probability of its event,
\[
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} - a \cdot {\Pr\mathopen{}\left(X \ge a\right)\mathclose{}}
= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X - a \cdot \mathbb{1}_{{\mathopen{}\left\{X \ge a\right\}\mathclose{}}}\right]\mathclose{}}
\ge 0
\]
and dividing by \(a &gt; 0\) gives the claim.










RemarkRemark






Remark. The inequality carries Markov’s name by convention. Chebyshev’s inequality, which follows from it, is older [5], [6]; see [7] for the history of the attribution. A modern treatment is in [8].










LemTheorem 4 (Chebyshev’s inequality)






Theorem 4 (Chebyshev’s inequality) Let \(X\) be a random variable with mean \(\mu\) and finite variance \(\sigma^2&gt; 0\). Then for every \(k &gt; 0\) [9]:
\[
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|X - \mu\right|\mathclose{} \ge k\sigma\right)\mathclose{}} \le \frac{1}{k^2}
\]










ProofProof






Proof. The random variable \((X - \mu)^2\) is nonnegative, and its expectation is \(\sigma^2\). Because \(\mathopen{}\left|X - \mu\right|\mathclose{} \ge k\sigma\) exactly when \((X - \mu)^2 \ge k^2\sigma^2\), Theorem 3 gives
\[
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|X - \mu\right|\mathclose{} \ge k\sigma\right)\mathclose{}}
= {\Pr\mathopen{}\left((X - \mu)^2 \ge k^2\sigma^2\right)\mathclose{}}
\le \frac{\sigma^2}{k^2\sigma^2}
= \frac{1}{k^2}
\]










RemarkRemark






Remark. Theorem 4 needs only a mean and a variance, not a distribution, so it holds for every distribution that has them. That generality makes it loose: for a normal distribution, the chance of landing more than two standard deviations from the mean is about 5%, but the bound at \(k = 2\) is 25%.










RemarkRemark






Remark. Bienaymé derived this bound in 1853 [5], and Chebyshev proved a more general form in 1867 [6]. A modern treatment is in [8].










ExmExample 3 (How loose is Chebyshev’s bound for car horsepower?)






Example 3 (How loose is Chebyshev’s bound for car horsepower?) Take the 392 horsepower values in the Auto data described in Example 2 as the whole population, and draw one car at random. For each \(k\), the code below counts the share of cars whose horsepower is at least \(k\) standard deviations from the mean, and sets it beside the bound \(1/k^2\) from Theorem 4.

hp &lt;- read.csv("data/auto.csv")$horsepower
mu &lt;- mean(hp)
sigma &lt;- sqrt(mean((hp - mu)^2))

k &lt;- c(1.5, 2, 3)
share_beyond &lt;- sapply(k, function(kk) mean(abs(hp - mu) &gt;= kk * sigma))
data.frame(k = k, share_of_cars = share_beyond, chebyshev_bound = 1 / k^2)






The bound holds at every \(k\), as Theorem 4 guarantees. At \(k = 2\) the share of cars is 4.3%, against a bound of 25%, and at \(k = 3\) it is 1.3% against 11.1%. The bound is loose because it must hold for every distribution with this mean and variance.










ExrExercise 1 (How many cars to estimate mean fuel economy)






Exercise 1 (How many cars to estimate mean fuel economy) Let \(X_1, \ldots, X_n\) be IID draws of the fuel economy (mpg) of a car chosen at random from the Auto data, with population mean \(\mu\) and variance \(\sigma^2\), and let \({{\bar{X}}_{n}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^nX_i\).

Use Theorem 2 and Theorem 4 to show that for every \(\epsilon &gt; 0\),
\[
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|{{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\right|\mathclose{} \ge \epsilon\right)\mathclose{}} \le \frac{\sigma^2}{n\epsilon^2}
\]
Compute \(\sigma^2\) for the mpg column of data/auto.csv, treating the 392 cars as the whole population. Find the smallest \(n\) for which the bound in (a) guarantees \({\Pr\mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|{{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\right|\mathclose{} \ge 1\right)\mathclose{}} \le 0.05\).
Simulate 10,000 samples of that size drawn with replacement, and count how many sample means miss \(\mu\) by 1 or more. Why is the count so far below the 5% the bound allows?











SolutionSolution 1






Solution 1. 

By Theorem 2, \({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(S_n\right)\mathclose{}} = n\sigma^2\) for \(S_n \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\sum_{i=1}^nX_i\), so \({{\bar{X}}_{n}} = S_n/n\) has mean \(\mu\) and variance \(\sigma^2/n\) (a constant factor \(c\) scales a variance by \(c^2\)). Apply Theorem 4 to \({{\bar{X}}_{n}}\) with \(k\sigma/\sqrt{n} = \epsilon\), that is, \(k = \epsilon\sqrt{n}/\sigma\), so \(1/k^2 = \sigma^2/(n\epsilon^2)\).
Setting \(\sigma^2/(n \cdot 1^2) \le 0.05\) gives \(n \ge 20\sigma^2\).
See the code below.


mpg &lt;- read.csv("data/auto.csv")$mpg
mu &lt;- mean(mpg)
sigma2 &lt;- mean((mpg - mu)^2)
n_min &lt;- ceiling(20 * sigma2)

set.seed(571)
xbar &lt;- replicate(10000, mean(sample(mpg, n_min, replace = TRUE)))
misses &lt;- sum(abs(xbar - mu) &gt;= 1)
normal_approx &lt;- 2 * pnorm(-1 / sqrt(sigma2 / n_min))
c(variance = sigma2, n = n_min, misses = misses, normal_approx = normal_approx)
#&gt;      variance             n        misses normal_approx 
#&gt;  6.076274e+01  1.216000e+03  0.000000e+00  7.694705e-06

The population variance of mpg is 60.8, so \(n = 1216\) cars suffice. Out of 10,000 samples of that size, 0 sample means missed \(\mu\) by 1 or more, against the 5% (500 samples) the bound allows. The bound is loose because the sample mean of this many cars is nearly normal (Theorem 1), and the normal approximation puts the chance of a miss at about 7.7^{-6}; Chebyshev’s inequality must hold for every distribution, as in Example 3.










LemTheorem 5 (Chebyshev’s inequality for a sample mean)






Theorem 5 (Chebyshev’s inequality for a sample mean) Let \(X_1, \ldots, X_n\) be IID random variables with population mean \(\mu\) and finite variance \(\sigma^2&gt; 0\), and let \({{\bar{X}}_{n}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^nX_i\). Then for every \(\epsilon &gt; 0\):
\[
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|{{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\right|\mathclose{} \ge \epsilon\right)\mathclose{}} \le \frac{\sigma^2}{n\epsilon^2}
\]










ProofProof






Proof. This is the solution to Exercise 1 (a), worked in Solution 1. It applies Theorem 4 to \({{\bar{X}}_{n}}\), whose variance is \(\sigma^2/n\) by Corollary 2.










LemLemma 1 (Hoeffding’s lemma)






Lemma 1 (Hoeffding’s lemma) Let \(X\) be a random variable with \(0 \le X \le 1\) and mean \(\mu\). Then for every real number \(s\),
\[
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[e^{s(X - \mu)}\right]\mathclose{}} \le e^{s^2/8}.
\]










ProofProof






Proof. First bound \(e^{sX}\) by a line. The function \(x \mapsto e^{x}\) is convex, and for \(0 \le X \le 1\) the number \(sX\) is the weighted average \((1 - X) \cdot 0 + X \cdot s\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
e^{sX}
&\le (1 - X) e^{0} + X e^{s}
&& \text{(convexity of the exponential)} \\
&= 1 - X + X e^{s}
&& \text{(} e^{0} = 1 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Take the expectation of both sides, which preserves the inequality:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[e^{sX}\right]\mathclose{}}
&\le 1 - {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} + {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} e^{s}
&& \text{(linearity of expectation)} \\
&= 1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}
&& \text{(definition of } \mu\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The number \(1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}\) is a weighted average of \(1\) and \(e^{s}\) with weights \(1 - \mu\) and \(\mu\), so it is positive. Multiply by the constant \(e^{-s\mu}\) and write the result as an exponential:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[e^{s(X - \mu)}\right]\mathclose{}}
&= e^{-s\mu} {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[e^{sX}\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(} e^{-s\mu} \text{ is a constant)} \\
&\le e^{-s\mu} \mathopen{}\left(1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(the bound above)} \\
&= e^{L(s)}
&& \text{(define } L(s) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}-s\mu+ \log\mathopen{}\left(1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}\right)\mathclose{} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
It remains to show \(L(s) \le s^2/8\). Write
\[
p(s) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{\mu e^{s}}{1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}},
\]
which lies in \([0, 1]\). The first derivative of \(L\) is
\[
\begin{aligned}
L'(s)
&= -\mu+ \frac{\mu e^{s}}{1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}}
&& \text{(chain rule for the logarithm)} \\
&= -\mu+ p(s)
&& \text{(definition of } p \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The derivative of \(p\) is
\[
\begin{aligned}
p'(s)
&= \frac{\mu e^{s}\mathopen{}\left(1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}\right)\mathclose{} - \mu e^{s} \cdot \mu e^{s}}{\mathopen{}\left(1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}\right)\mathclose{}^2}
&& \text{(quotient rule)} \\
&= \frac{\mu e^{s}\mathopen{}\left(1 - \mu\right)\mathclose{}}{\mathopen{}\left(1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}\right)\mathclose{}^2}
&& \text{(the } \mu^2 e^{2s} \text{ terms cancel)} \\
&= p(s)\mathopen{}\left(1 - p(s)\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(} 1 - p(s) = \tfrac{1 - \mu}{1 - \mu+ \mu e^{s}} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(L''(s) = p'(s) = p(s)\mathopen{}\left(1 - p(s)\right)\mathclose{}\). Completing the square gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
p(s)\mathopen{}\left(1 - p(s)\right)\mathclose{}
&= \frac{1}{4} - \mathopen{}\left(p(s) - \frac{1}{2}\right)\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(complete the square)} \\
&\le \frac{1}{4}
&& \text{(a square is not negative)}
\end{aligned}
\]
At \(s = 0\), \(L(0) = 0 + \log 1 = 0\) and \(L'(0) = -\mu+ \mu= 0\). By Taylor’s theorem with the Lagrange remainder, there is a number \(\xi\) between \(0\) and \(s\) with
\[
\begin{aligned}
L(s)
&= L(0) + L'(0)\, s + \frac{L''(\xi)}{2} s^2
&& \text{(Taylor's theorem)} \\
&= \frac{L''(\xi)}{2} s^2
&& \text{(} L(0) = L'(0) = 0 \text{)} \\
&\le \frac{s^2}{8}
&& \text{(} L''(\xi) \le \tfrac{1}{4} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[e^{s(X - \mu)}\right]\mathclose{}} \le e^{L(s)} \le e^{s^2/8}\).










RemarkRemark






Remark. The bound on the moment generating function \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[e^{s(X - \mu)}\right]\mathclose{}}\) is the step of Hoeffding’s 1963 paper [10] that the literature calls Hoeffding’s lemma. The next result uses it to bound the tails of a sample mean.










LemTheorem 6 (Hoeffding’s inequality)






Theorem 6 (Hoeffding’s inequality) Let \(X_1, \ldots, X_n\) be IID random variables with \(0 \le X_i \le 1\) and population mean \(\mu\), and let \({{\bar{X}}_{n}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^nX_i\). Then for every \(\varepsilon&gt; 0\) [11],
\[
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\ge \varepsilon\right)\mathclose{}} \le e^{-2n\varepsilon^2}.
\tag{1}\]










ProofProof






Proof. Let the free parameter be \(s &gt; 0\). The event \({\mathopen{}\left\{{{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\ge \varepsilon\right\}\mathclose{}}\) is the event \({\mathopen{}\left\{e^{sn({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu)} \ge e^{sn\varepsilon}\right\}\mathclose{}}\), because the exponential is increasing and \(sn &gt; 0\). The random variable \(e^{sn({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu)}\) is positive, so Markov’s inequality applies:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\ge \varepsilon\right)\mathclose{}}
&= {\Pr\mathopen{}\left(e^{sn({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu)} \ge e^{sn\varepsilon}\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(the exponential is increasing)} \\
&\le e^{-sn\varepsilon} {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[e^{sn({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu)}\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(Markov's inequality)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Rewrite the exponent as a sum, so that the exponential becomes a product:
\[
\begin{aligned}
e^{sn({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu)}
&= e^{s\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i - \mu)}
&& \text{(} n{{\bar{X}}_{n}} = \sum_{i=1}^nX_i \text{)} \\
&= \prod_{i=1}^n e^{s(X_i - \mu)}
&& \text{(} e^{a + b} = e^{a} e^{b} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The factors are functions of independent random variables, so they are independent, and the expectation of a product of independent random variables is the product of their expectations (the expectation of a product of independent variables, applied one factor at a time). Then apply Lemma 1 to each factor:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[e^{sn({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu)}\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \prod_{i=1}^n {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[e^{s(X_i - \mu)}\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(independence)} \\
&\le \prod_{i=1}^n e^{s^2/8}
&& \text{(Hoeffding's lemma)} \\
&= e^{ns^2/8}
&& \text{(} n \text{ equal factors)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Combining the two displays, for every \(s &gt; 0\),
\[
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\ge \varepsilon\right)\mathclose{}} \le e^{-sn\varepsilon+ ns^2/8}.
\]
The right side is smallest where the exponent \(-sn\varepsilon+ ns^2/8\) is smallest. The exponent is a quadratic in \(s\) with a positive leading coefficient, so it has one minimum, where its derivative is zero. Its derivative in \(s\) is \(-n\varepsilon+ ns/4\), which is zero at \(s = 4\varepsilon\), and \(4\varepsilon&gt; 0\) is allowed. There the exponent is
\[
\begin{aligned}
-4\varepsilon\cdot n\varepsilon+ \frac{n(4\varepsilon)^2}{8}
&= -4n\varepsilon^2 + 2n\varepsilon^2
&& \text{(substitute } s = 4\varepsilon\text{)} \\
&= -2n\varepsilon^2
&& \text{(combine like terms)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is the claim.










CorCorollary 3 (Hoeffding’s inequality, two-sided)






Corollary 3 (Hoeffding’s inequality, two-sided) Under the assumptions of Theorem 6, for every \(\varepsilon&gt; 0\),
\[
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|{{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\right|\mathclose{} \ge \varepsilon\right)\mathclose{}} \le 2e^{-2n\varepsilon^2}.
\tag{2}\]










ProofProof






Proof. The variables \(1 - X_1, \ldots, 1 - X_n\) are IID, lie in \([0, 1]\), and have mean \(1 - \mu\). Their sample mean is \(1 - {{\bar{X}}_{n}}\), so Theorem 6 applied to them gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left(\mu- {{\bar{X}}_{n}} \ge \varepsilon\right)\mathclose{}}
&= {\Pr\mathopen{}\left((1 - {{\bar{X}}_{n}}) - (1 - \mu) \ge \varepsilon\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(rearrange the event)} \\
&\le e^{-2n\varepsilon^2}
&& \text{(Hoeffding's inequality for } 1 - X_i \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The event \({\mathopen{}\left\{\mathopen{}\left|{{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\right|\mathclose{} \ge \varepsilon\right\}\mathclose{}}\) is the union of \({\mathopen{}\left\{{{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\ge \varepsilon\right\}\mathclose{}}\) and \({\mathopen{}\left\{\mu- {{\bar{X}}_{n}} \ge \varepsilon\right\}\mathclose{}}\). The probability of a union is at most the sum of the probabilities, so
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\Pr\mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|{{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\right|\mathclose{} \ge \varepsilon\right)\mathclose{}}
&\le {\Pr\mathopen{}\left({{\bar{X}}_{n}} - \mu\ge \varepsilon\right)\mathclose{}} + {\Pr\mathopen{}\left(\mu- {{\bar{X}}_{n}} \ge \varepsilon\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(union bound)} \\
&\le e^{-2n\varepsilon^2} + e^{-2n\varepsilon^2}
&& \text{(Hoeffding's inequality, twice)} \\
&= 2e^{-2n\varepsilon^2}
&& \text{(combine like terms)}
\end{aligned}
\]










RemarkRemark






Remark. Hoeffding stated the inequality for sums of independent bounded random variables, with different bounds for each term [10]. This page states the case of the sample mean of IID variables in \([0, 1]\), which is the case that bounds the gap between a sample proportion and the probability it estimates. For an indicator variable, \(\mu\) is the probability of the event and \({{\bar{X}}_{n}}\) is the share of the \(n\) draws in which it occurs.










ExmExample 4 (How loose is Hoeffding’s bound for the share of efficient cars?)






Example 4 (How loose is Hoeffding’s bound for the share of efficient cars?) Take the 392 cars in the Auto data described in Example 2 as the whole population, and call a car efficient if its fuel economy (mpg) is above \(25\). Let \(X_i = 1\) if the \(i\)th car drawn at random is efficient and \(X_i = 0\) otherwise, so \(\mu\) is the share of efficient cars and \({{\bar{X}}_{n}}\) is the share in a sample of \(n\) draws. Each \(X_i\) lies in \([0, 1]\), and the draws are made with replacement, so the \(X_i\) are IID and Corollary 3 applies. The code below draws \(10{,}000\) samples of each size, computes the proportion of samples whose share misses \(\mu\) by at least \(\varepsilon= 0.1\), and sets that proportion beside the bound \(2e^{-2n\varepsilon^2}\) of Equation 2 and the bound \(\sigma^2/(n\varepsilon^2)\) that Chebyshev’s inequality gives (Theorem 5).

mpg &lt;- read.csv("data/auto.csv")$mpg
efficient &lt;- as.numeric(mpg &gt; 25)
mu &lt;- mean(efficient)
sigma2 &lt;- mean((efficient - mu)^2)
tol &lt;- 0.1

n &lt;- c(50, 100, 200, 400)
set.seed(571)
share_missed &lt;- sapply(n, function(nn) {
  xbar &lt;- replicate(10000, mean(sample(efficient, nn, replace = TRUE)))
  mean(abs(xbar - mu) &gt;= tol)
})
hoeffding_bound &lt;- pmin(1, 2 * exp(-2 * n * tol^2))
chebyshev_bound &lt;- pmin(1, sigma2 / (n * tol^2))
data.frame(
  n = n,
  share_missed = share_missed,
  hoeffding_bound = hoeffding_bound,
  chebyshev_bound = chebyshev_bound
)






The simulated proportions lie below both bounds at every sample size, as they should: the bounds apply to the true probabilities these proportions estimate. The share of efficient cars is 0.398. For \(n = 50\) and \(n = 100\) Chebyshev’s bound is the smaller of the two, but the Hoeffding bound falls exponentially in \(n\) while the Chebyshev bound falls only like \(1/n\), so at \(n = 200\) and \(n = 400\) Hoeffding’s is the smaller. At \(n = 400\) the Hoeffding bound is 0.00067, against 0.06 for Chebyshev, and 0 of the 10,000 samples missed. Both bounds are loose because they must hold for every distribution of this kind, not only for this one.

2 The binomial distribution






DefDefinition 1 (Binomial distribution)






Definition 1 (Binomial distribution) A discrete random variable \(X\) has the binomial distribution with number of trials \(n \in {\mathopen{}\left\{1, 2, \dots\right\}\mathclose{}}\) and event probability \(\pi\in [0, 1]\), written \(X \sim {\operatorname{Bin}\mathopen{}\left(n, \pi\right)\mathclose{}}\) [1], if:
\[
\mathbb{P}(X = x) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\binom{n}{x} \pi^x (1 - \pi)^{n - x},
\quad x \in {\mathopen{}\left\{0, 1, \dots, n\right\}\mathclose{}}
\tag{1}\]
where \(\binom{n}{x} = \frac{n!}{x!\,(n - x)!}\) is the number of ways to choose \(x\) of the \(n\) trials.










RemarkRemark






Remark. Casella and Berger [2, Sec. 3.2] defines the binomial distribution the same way. With \(n = 1\), Equation 1 is the Bernoulli PMF: \({\operatorname{Bin}\mathopen{}\left(1, \pi\right)\mathclose{}}\) and \(\operatorname{Ber}(\pi)\) are the same distribution.










ExmExample 2 (Two heads in three flips)






Example 2 (Two heads in three flips) Let \(X \sim {\operatorname{Bin}\mathopen{}\left(3, 1/2\right)\mathclose{}}\) be the number of heads in three flips of a fair coin. By Equation 1:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(X = 2)
&= \binom{3}{2} \mathopen{}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\mathclose{}^2 \mathopen{}\left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right)\mathclose{}^{3 - 2}
&& \text{(binomial PMF with } n = 3,\ \pi= 1/2,\ x = 2 \text{)} \\
&= \frac{3!}{2!\,1!} \mathopen{}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\mathclose{}^2 \mathopen{}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\mathclose{}^1
&& \text{(definition of } \tbinom{3}{2} \text{; } 1 - 1/2 = 1/2 \text{)} \\
&= 3 \cdot\frac{1}{4} \cdot\frac{1}{2}
&& \text{(evaluate the factorials and powers)} \\
&= \frac{3}{8}
&& \text{(multiply)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The three outcomes with two heads are \(HHT\), \(HTH\), and \(THH\), each with probability \(1/8\).










ExmExample 3 (Draws without replacement are not binomial)






Example 3 (Draws without replacement are not binomial) An urn holds two red balls and two blue balls. Draw two balls without replacement, and let \(X\) be the number of red balls drawn. Each draw is red with probability \(1/2\), but \(X \not\sim {\operatorname{Bin}\mathopen{}\left(2, 1/2\right)\mathclose{}}\), because the draws are not independent:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(X = 2)
&= \mathbb{P}(\text{first red}) \cdot\mathbb{P}(\text{second red} \mid \text{first red})
&& \text{(multiplication rule)} \\
&= \frac{2}{4} \cdot\frac{1}{3}
&& \text{(one red ball is left among three)} \\
&= \frac{1}{6}
&& \text{(multiply)}
\end{aligned}
\]
whereas, by Equation 1, a \({\operatorname{Bin}\mathopen{}\left(2, 1/2\right)\mathclose{}}\) random variable has
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(X = 2)
&= \binom{2}{2} \mathopen{}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\mathclose{}^2 \mathopen{}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\mathclose{}^0
&& \text{(binomial PMF with } n = 2,\ \pi= 1/2,\ x = 2 \text{)} \\
&= \frac{1}{4}
&& \text{(evaluate)}
\end{aligned}
\]










LemTheorem 1 (A sum of IID Bernoulli variables is binomial)






Theorem 1 (A sum of IID Bernoulli variables is binomial) If \(X_1, \ldots, X_n\) are IID \(\operatorname{Ber}(\pi)\) random variables, then their sum \(S = \sum_{i=1}^nX_i\) has the \({\operatorname{Bin}\mathopen{}\left(n, \pi\right)\mathclose{}}\) distribution.










ProofProof






Proof. Each \(X_i\) is \(0\) or \(1\), so \(S\) takes values in \({\mathopen{}\left\{0, 1, \dots, n\right\}\mathclose{}}\). Let \(x \in {\mathopen{}\left\{0, 1, \dots, n\right\}\mathclose{}}\) be a value of \(S\), and let \(A_x\) be the set of sequences \((s_1, \ldots, s_n) \in {\mathopen{}\left\{0, 1\right\}\mathclose{}}^n\) with exactly \(x\) ones. The event \({\mathopen{}\left\{S = x\right\}\mathclose{}}\) is the disjoint union of the events \({\mathopen{}\left\{X_1 = s_1, \ldots, X_n = s_n\right\}\mathclose{}}\) over \((s_1, \ldots, s_n) \in A_x\). For one such sequence:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(X_1 = s_1, \ldots, X_n = s_n)
&= \prod_{i=1}^n \mathbb{P}(X_i = s_i)
&& \text{(independence)} \\
&= \prod_{i=1}^n \pi^{s_i} (1 - \pi)^{1 - s_i}
&& \text{(Bernoulli PMF)} \\
&= \pi^{\sum_{i=1}^ns_i} (1 - \pi)^{\sum_{i=1}^n(1 - s_i)}
&& \text{(collect exponents)} \\
&= \pi^x (1 - \pi)^{n - x}
&& \text{(} \textstyle\sum_{i=1}^ns_i = x \text{ for a sequence in } A_x \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Choosing which \(x\) of the \(n\) positions hold the ones determines the sequence, so \(A_x\) has \(\binom{n}{x}\) elements. Therefore:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(S = x)
&= \sum_{(s_1, \ldots, s_n) \in A_x} \mathbb{P}(X_1 = s_1, \ldots, X_n = s_n)
&& \text{(additivity over disjoint events)} \\
&= \sum_{(s_1, \ldots, s_n) \in A_x} \pi^x (1 - \pi)^{n - x}
&& \text{(probability of one sequence)} \\
&= \binom{n}{x} \pi^x (1 - \pi)^{n - x}
&& \text{(} A_x \text{ has } \tbinom{n}{x} \text{ elements)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is Equation 1.










ExmExample 4 (Counting treatment responders)






Example 4 (Counting treatment responders) Suppose five patients each respond to a treatment with probability \(0.2\), independently of one another, and let \(X_i = 1\) if patient \(i\) responds and \(X_i = 0\) otherwise. By Theorem 1, the number of responders \(S = \sum_{i=1}^5 X_i\) is \({\operatorname{Bin}\mathopen{}\left(5, 0.2\right)\mathclose{}}\), so the probability that nobody responds is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(S = 0)
&= \binom{5}{0} (0.2)^0 (1 - 0.2)^{5 - 0}
&& \text{(binomial PMF with } n = 5,\ \pi= 0.2,\ x = 0 \text{)} \\
&= 1 \cdot 1 \cdot(0.8)^5
&& \text{(} \tbinom{5}{0} = 1 \text{ and } (0.2)^0 = 1 \text{)} \\
&= 0.32768
&& \text{(evaluate the power)}
\end{aligned}
\]










LemTheorem 2 (Mean and variance of the binomial distribution)






Theorem 2 (Mean and variance of the binomial distribution) If \(X \sim {\operatorname{Bin}\mathopen{}\left(n, \pi\right)\mathclose{}}\), then:

\({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} = n\pi\)
\({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(X\right)\mathclose{}} = n\pi(1 - \pi)\)











ProofProof






Proof. The mean and variance of \(X\) depend only on its distribution, so by Theorem 1 we can compute them for \(S = \sum_{i=1}^nX_i\), where \(X_1, \ldots, X_n\) are IID \(\operatorname{Ber}(\pi)\). For the mean, apply linearity of expectation \(n - 1\) times, then the Bernoulli mean:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[S\right]\mathclose{}}
&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[\sum_{i=1}^nX_i\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(definition of } S \text{)} \\
&= \sum_{i=1}^n{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X_i\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(linearity of expectation)} \\
&= \sum_{i=1}^n\pi
&& \text{(Bernoulli mean)} \\
&= n\pi
&& \text{(sum of } n \text{ copies of } \pi\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For the variance, apply the variance of a linear combination with \(a_1 = \cdots = a_n = 1\), then independent components give a diagonal variance matrix, then the Bernoulli variance:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(S\right)\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n {\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(X_i, X_j\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(variance of a linear combination with } a_i = 1 \text{)} \\
&= \sum_{i=1}^n{\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(X_i, X_i\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(} {\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(X_i, X_j\right)\mathclose{}} = 0 \text{ for } i \neq j \text{, by independence)} \\
&= \sum_{i=1}^n{\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(X_i\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(} {\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(X_i, X_i\right)\mathclose{}} = {\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(X_i\right)\mathclose{}} \text{)} \\
&= \sum_{i=1}^n\pi(1 - \pi)
&& \text{(Bernoulli variance)} \\
&= n\pi(1 - \pi)
&& \text{(sum of } n \text{ copies of } \pi(1 - \pi) \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 5 (Expected number of responders)






Example 5 (Expected number of responders) In Example 4, \(S \sim {\operatorname{Bin}\mathopen{}\left(5, 0.2\right)\mathclose{}}\). By Theorem 2:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[S\right]\mathclose{}} &= 5 \cdot 0.2 && \text{(} n\pi\text{)} \\
&= 1 && \text{(multiply)} \\
{\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(S\right)\mathclose{}} &= 5 \cdot 0.2 \cdot(1 - 0.2) && \text{(} n\pi(1 - \pi) \text{)} \\
&= 0.8 && \text{(multiply)}
\end{aligned}
\]
On average, one of the five patients responds.

4 The Negative-Binomial distribution






DefDefinition 6 (Negative binomial distribution)






Definition 6 (Negative binomial distribution) A random variable \(Y\) has the negative binomial distribution with mean \(\mu&gt; 0\) and overdispersion parameter \(\rho&gt; 0\), written \(Y \sim \operatorname{NegBin}(\mu, \rho)\) [4], if, for \(y \in {\mathopen{}\left\{0, 1, 2, \dots\right\}\mathclose{}}\):
\[
\mathbb{P}(Y=y)
\stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{\mu^y}{y!}
\cdot \frac{\Gamma(\rho+ y)}{\Gamma(\rho) \cdot (\rho+ \mu)^y}
\cdot \mathopen{}\left(1+\frac{\mu}{\rho}\right)\mathclose{}^{-\rho}
\]
where \(\Gamma\) is the gamma function, which satisfies \(\Gamma(x) = (x-1)!\) for positive integers \(x\).










LemTheorem 10 (The negative binomial converges to the Poisson)






Theorem 10 (The negative binomial converges to the Poisson) Let the population mean be \(\mu&gt; 0\), and let \(Y_\rho\sim \operatorname{NegBin}(\mu, \rho)\). Then for each \(y \in {\mathopen{}\left\{0, 1, 2, \dots\right\}\mathclose{}}\), as \(\rho\rightarrow \infty\), the negative binomial PMF converges to the Poisson PMF (Equation 2):
\[\lim_{\rho\rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{P}(Y_\rho= y) = \frac{\mu^{y} e^{-\mu}}{y!}\]










ProofProof






Proof. Choose a value \(y\) of \(Y_\rho\). In Definition 6, the first factor \(\mu^y / y!\) does not depend on \(\rho\). For the second factor, \(\Gamma(\rho+ y) = \Gamma(\rho) \prod_{k=0}^{y-1} (\rho+ k)\), by applying \(\Gamma(x + 1) = x\,\Gamma(x)\) \(y\) times (for \(y = 0\), the product is empty and equals 1), so:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\Gamma(\rho+ y)}{\Gamma(\rho) \cdot (\rho+ \mu)^y}
&= \frac{\Gamma(\rho) \prod_{k=0}^{y-1} (\rho+ k)}{\Gamma(\rho) \cdot (\rho+ \mu)^y}
&& (\Gamma(x + 1) = x\,\Gamma(x) \text{, applied } y \text{ times}) \\
&= \prod_{k=0}^{y-1} \frac{\rho+ k}{\rho+ \mu}
&& (\text{cancel } \Gamma(\rho) \text{; one factor of } \rho+ \mu\text{ per } k) \\
&= \prod_{k=0}^{y-1} \frac{1 + k/\rho}{1 + \mu/\rho}
&& (\text{divide each numerator and denominator by } \rho) \\
&\rightarrow \prod_{k=0}^{y-1} \frac{1 + 0}{1 + 0}
&& (k/\rho\rightarrow 0 \text{ and } \mu/\rho\rightarrow 0 \text{; finitely many factors}) \\
&= 1
&& (\text{simplify})
\end{aligned}
\]
For the third factor, write \(x \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mu/\rho\), so \(x \rightarrow 0\) as \(\rho\rightarrow \infty\), and \(\rho= \mu/x\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\log\mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left(1 + \frac{\mu}{\rho}\right)\mathclose{}^{-\rho}\right)\mathclose{}
&= -\rho\log\mathopen{}\left(1 + \frac{\mu}{\rho}\right)\mathclose{}
&& (\text{log of a power}) \\
&= -\mu\cdot \frac{\log(1 + x)}{x}
&& (\text{substitute } \rho= \mu/x) \\
&\rightarrow -\mu\cdot 1
&& (\textstyle\lim_{x \rightarrow 0} \log(1 + x)/x = 1 \text{, the derivative of } \log(1 + x) \text{ at } x = 0) \\
&= -\mu
&& (\text{simplify})
\end{aligned}
\]
so, since the exponential function is continuous, \(\mathopen{}\left(1 + \mu/\rho\right)\mathclose{}^{-\rho} \rightarrow {\operatorname{exp}\mathopen{}\left\{-\mu\right\}\mathclose{}}\). Each of the three factors has a limit, so their product does too:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{\rho\rightarrow \infty} \mathbb{P}(Y_\rho= y)
&= \frac{\mu^y}{y!} \cdot 1 \cdot {\operatorname{exp}\mathopen{}\left\{-\mu\right\}\mathclose{}}
&& (\text{limit of a product of convergent factors}) \\
&= \frac{\mu^{y} e^{-\mu}}{y!}
&& (\text{rearrange})
\end{aligned}
\]
which is the \(\operatorname{Pois}(\mu)\) PMF.










LemTheorem 11 (Mean and variance of the negative binomial distribution)






Theorem 11 (Mean and variance of the negative binomial distribution) If \(Y \sim \operatorname{NegBin}(\mu, \rho)\), then:

\(\mathbb{E}[Y] = \mu\)
\({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y\right)\mathclose{}} = \mu+ \frac{\mu^2}{\rho} &gt; \mu\)











ProofProof






Proof. The negative binomial distribution is a gamma mixture of Poisson distributions: if \(\Lambda\) has the gamma density \(g({\lambda}) = \frac{(\rho/\mu)^\rho}{\Gamma(\rho)} {\lambda}^{\rho- 1} e^{-\rho{\lambda}/\mu}\) for \({\lambda}&gt; 0\), which has mean \(\mu\) and variance \(\mu^2/\rho\) [2], and \(Y \mid \Lambda= {\lambda}\sim \operatorname{Pois}({\lambda})\), then \(Y \sim \operatorname{NegBin}(\mu, \rho)\). To check this, integrate the joint density over \({\lambda}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(Y = y)
&= \int_0^\infty \frac{{\lambda}^y e^{-{\lambda}}}{y!} \cdot\frac{(\rho/\mu)^\rho}{\Gamma(\rho)} {\lambda}^{\rho- 1} e^{-\rho{\lambda}/\mu}\,d{\lambda}
&& (\text{marginalize over } \Lambda) \\
&= \frac{(\rho/\mu)^\rho}{y!\,\Gamma(\rho)} \int_0^\infty {\lambda}^{y + \rho- 1} e^{-{\lambda}(1 + \rho/\mu)}\,d{\lambda}
&& (\text{collect powers of } {\lambda}\text{ and exponents}) \\
&= \frac{(\rho/\mu)^\rho}{y!\,\Gamma(\rho)} \cdot\frac{\Gamma(y + \rho)}{(1 + \rho/\mu)^{y + \rho}}
&& (\textstyle\int_0^\infty {\lambda}^{a-1} e^{-b{\lambda}}\,d{\lambda}= \Gamma(a)/b^a) \\
&= \frac{\Gamma(y + \rho)}{y!\,\Gamma(\rho)} \mathopen{}\left(\frac{\rho}{\mu}\right)\mathclose{}^\rho\mathopen{}\left(\frac{\mu}{\mu+ \rho}\right)\mathclose{}^{y + \rho}
&& (1 + \rho/\mu= (\mu+ \rho)/\mu) \\
&= \frac{\Gamma(y + \rho)}{y!\,\Gamma(\rho)} \mathopen{}\left(\frac{\rho}{\mu+ \rho}\right)\mathclose{}^{\rho} \mathopen{}\left(\frac{\mu}{\mu+ \rho}\right)\mathclose{}^{y}
&& (\text{combine the } \mu^\rho\text{ factors}) \\
&= \frac{\mu^y}{y!} \cdot\frac{\Gamma(\rho+ y)}{\Gamma(\rho)\,(\rho+ \mu)^y} \cdot\mathopen{}\left(1 + \frac{\mu}{\rho}\right)\mathclose{}^{-\rho}
&& (\text{rearrange into the form of the definition})
\end{aligned}
\]
Then, by the law of iterated expectations and the law of total variance, using
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid \Lambda\right]\mathclose{}} &= \operatorname{Var}(Y \mid \Lambda) \\
&= \Lambda
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 4):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}[Y]
&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid \Lambda\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}}
&& (\text{law of iterated expectations}) \\
&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[\Lambda\right]\mathclose{}}
&& ({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid \Lambda\right]\mathclose{}} = \Lambda) \\
&= \mu
&& (\text{mean of the gamma distribution}) \\
{\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y\right)\mathclose{}}
&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y \mid \Lambda\right)\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}} + {\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid \Lambda\right]\mathclose{}}\right)\mathclose{}}
&& (\text{law of total variance}) \\
&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[\Lambda\right]\mathclose{}} + {\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(\Lambda\right)\mathclose{}}
&& ({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y \mid \Lambda\right)\mathclose{}} = \Lambda\text{ and } {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid \Lambda\right]\mathclose{}} = \Lambda) \\
&= \mu+ \frac{\mu^2}{\rho}
&& (\text{mean and variance of the gamma distribution})
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(\mu^2/\rho&gt; 0\) gives \({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y\right)\mathclose{}} &gt; \mu\).










ExmExample 13 (Overdispersion relative to the Poisson)






Example 13 (Overdispersion relative to the Poisson) With \(\mu= 4\) and \(\rho= 2\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y\right)\mathclose{}} &= 4 + 16/2 \\
&= 12,
\end{aligned}
\]
three times the variance of a \(\operatorname{Pois}(4)\) count with the same mean.

1 Conditional distributions and expectations







DefDefinition 3 (Conditional probability mass function)






Definition 3 (Conditional probability mass function) Let \(X\) and \(Y\) be jointly distributed discrete random variables. The conditional probability mass function of \(Y\) given \(X = x\) (for values of \(x\) with \(\mathbb{P}(X = x) &gt; 0\)) is:
\[\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{\mathbb{P}(X = x,\, Y = y)}{\mathbb{P}(X = x)}\]










ExmExample 9 (Conditional PMF from a vaccine trial)






Example 9 (Conditional PMF from a vaccine trial) The vaccine dataset in the dobson package (Dobson and Barnett [8], Table 9.6) records the responses in a flu vaccine trial: \(X\) is treatment group (placebo or vaccine) and \(Y\) is the level of response to treatment (small, moderate, or large).


Show code
response_levels &lt;- c("small", "moderate", "large")
vaccine_tab &lt;-
  dobson::vaccine |&gt;
  dplyr::mutate(response = factor(response, levels = response_levels)) |&gt;
  xtabs(frequency ~ treatment + response, data = _)
pander::pander(as.data.frame.matrix(vaccine_tab))











 
