Show code
n_vaccine &lt;- sum(vaccine_tab)
n_placebo &lt;- vaccine_tab["placebo", ]


Choose one of the \(n = 73\) trial participants at random (a random choice), and let \((X, Y)\) be that participant’s treatment and response. The joint PMF of \((X, Y)\) is then the table of frequencies divided by the total \(n\) trial participants. By the marginal PMF theorem, the marginal probability \(\mathbb{P}(X = \text{placebo})\) is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(X = \text{placebo})
&= \mathbb{P}(X = \text{placebo},\, Y = \text{small})
+ \mathbb{P}(X = \text{placebo},\, Y = \text{moderate})
+ \mathbb{P}(X = \text{placebo},\, Y = \text{large})
&& \text{(marginal PMF from the joint PMF)}
\\&= \tfrac{25}{73}
+ \tfrac{8}{73}
+ \tfrac{5}{73}
&& \text{(read the table)}
\\&= \tfrac{38}{73}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
By Definition 3, the conditional PMF of \(Y\) given \(X = \text{placebo}\) is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(Y = \text{small} \mid X = \text{placebo})
&= \frac{\mathbb{P}(X = \text{placebo},\, Y = \text{small})}{\mathbb{P}(X = \text{placebo})}
&& \text{(definition of conditional PMF)}
\\&= \frac{25/73}{38/73}
&& \text{(substitute)}
\\&= \frac{25}{38}
&& \text{(cancel } 73 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










DefDefinition 4 (Conditional probability density function)






Definition 4 (Conditional probability density function) Let \(X\) and \(Y\) be jointly distributed continuous random variables with joint density \(\operatorname{p}(X = x,\, Y = y)\) and marginal density \(\operatorname{p}(X = x)\). The conditional probability density function of \(Y\) given \(X = x\) (for values of \(x\) with \(\operatorname{p}(X = x) &gt; 0\)) is:
\[\operatorname{p}(Y = y \mid X = x) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{\operatorname{p}(X = x,\, Y = y)}{\operatorname{p}(X = x)}\]










ExmExample 10 (Conditional PDF from a bivariate normal model of birthweight data)






Example 10 (Conditional PDF from a bivariate normal model of birthweight data) The birthweight dataset in the dobson package (Dobson and Barnett [8], Table 2.3) records gestational age (weeks) and birthweight (grams) for 12 boys and 12 girls. Let \(X\) be gestational age and \(Y\) be birthweight, pooling both sexes into \(n = 24\) observations.


