DefDefinition 7 (Conditional expectation function)






Definition 7 (Conditional expectation function) The conditional expectation function \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\) is the function (and hence random variable) of \(X\) obtained by evaluating \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\) at \(X\); specifically, \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}} = g(X)\) where \(g(x) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\).










ExmExample 13 (The conditional expectation function of the birthweight model)






Example 13 (The conditional expectation function of the birthweight model) Continuing Example 10 and Example 11, \((X, Y)\) (gestational age, birthweight) is modeled as bivariate normal. The general form of the conditional mean derived in Example 10, evaluated at an arbitrary \(x\) instead of just \(x=40\), gives the conditional expectation function directly:
\[
\begin{aligned}
g(x) &= \mu_Y + \rho\frac{\sigma_Y}{\sigma_X}(x-\mu_X)
\\&= -1484.9846 + 115.5283\, x
\end{aligned}
\]
which is exactly the fitted regression line (the general algebraic identity \(\mu_Y + \rho\frac{\sigma_Y}{\sigma_X}(x-\mu_X) = \mathopen{}\left(\mu_Y - \rho\frac{\sigma_Y}{\sigma_X}\mu_X\right)\mathclose{} + \rho\frac{\sigma_Y}{\sigma_X}x\) is what makes the bivariate-normal conditional mean linear in \(x\)). As a check, evaluating at \(x = 40\) recovers Example 11’s result:
\[g(40) = -1484.9846 + 115.5283 \times 40 = 3136.15 \text{ g}\]










DefDefinition 8 (Conditional expectation of a function of \(X\) and \(Y\))






Definition 8 (Conditional expectation of a function of \(X\) and \(Y\)) Let \(X\) and \(Y\) be jointly distributed random variables, \(h\) a function of two arguments, and \(x\) a value at which the conditional PMF or PDF of \(Y\) given \(X = x\) is defined (Definition 3, Definition 4, or Definition 6). The conditional expectation of \(h(X, Y)\) given \(X = x\) is, for discrete \(Y\):
\[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} h(x, y) \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)\]
and for continuous \(Y\):
\[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\int_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} h(x, y) \cdot\operatorname{p}(Y = y \mid X = x)\,dy\]
when the sum or integral converges absolutely. Evaluating this function of \(x\) at \(X\) gives the random variable \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\).










ExmExample 14 (The second flip, given the first)






Example 14 (The second flip, given the first) In the joint PMF of two coin flips, write \(X\) for the first flip and \(Y\) for the total number of heads. Given \(X = 1\), \(Y\) is \(1\) or \(2\) with conditional probability \(1/2\) each, and \(Y - X\) is the second flip. With \(h(x, y) = (y - x)^2\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[(Y - X)^2 \mid X = 1\right]\mathclose{}}
&= (1 - 1)^2 \cdot\tfrac{1}{2} + (2 - 1)^2 \cdot\tfrac{1}{2}
&& \text{(definition, with } x = 1 \text{)} \\
&= 0 + \tfrac{1}{2}
&& \text{(evaluate each term)} \\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]










CorCorollary 2 (With \(h(x, y) = y\), the general definition gives \({{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}}\))






Corollary 2 (With \(h(x, y) = y\), the general definition gives \({{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}}\)) For jointly distributed \(X\) and \(Y\) and \(h(x, y) = y\), Definition 8 gives the conditional expectation of \(Y\) given \(X = x\) from Definition 5 (when \(X\) and \(Y\) are both discrete or both continuous) or from Definition 6 (when one is discrete and the other continuous):
\[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}} = {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\]










ProofProof






Proof. For discrete \(Y\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} h(x, y) \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(conditional expectation of a function, discrete } Y \text{)} \\
&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(} h(x, y) = y \text{)} \\
&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(conditional expectation, discrete } Y \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The last step is Definition 5 when \(X\) is discrete and Definition 6 when \(X\) is continuous. For continuous \(Y\), the same steps hold with integrals over \(y\) in place of the sums and \(\operatorname{p}(Y = y \mid X = x)\) in place of \(\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)\).










LemTheorem 6 (Conditional LOTUS, discrete case)






Theorem 6 (Conditional LOTUS, discrete case) Let \(X\) and \(Y\) be jointly distributed discrete random variables, \(h\) a function of two arguments, \(W \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}h(X, Y)\), and \(x\) a value with \(\mathbb{P}(X = x) &gt; 0\). Then \(W\) is discrete, and \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\) from Definition 8 equals \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[W \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\) from Definition 5, with one sum converging absolutely exactly when the other does.










ProofProof






Proof. \(W\) is discrete because its range is contained in \({\mathopen{}\left\{h(u, y) : u \in \mathcal{R}(X),\, y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)\right\}\mathclose{}}\), which is countable. For each \(w \in \mathcal{R}(W)\), the event \({\mathopen{}\left\{X = x,\, W = w\right\}\mathclose{}}\) is the disjoint union of the events \({\mathopen{}\left\{X = x,\, Y = y\right\}\mathclose{}}\) over the values \(y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)\) with \(h(x, y) = w\), so:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(W = w \mid X = x)
&= \frac{\mathbb{P}(X = x,\, W = w)}{\mathbb{P}(X = x)}
&& \text{(conditional PMF of } W \text{)} \\
&= \frac{1}{\mathbb{P}(X = x)} \sum_{\substack{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y) \\ h(x, y) = w}} \mathbb{P}(X = x,\, Y = y)
&& \text{(countable additivity over the disjoint events } {\mathopen{}\left\{X = x,\, Y = y\right\}\mathclose{}} \text{)} \\
&= \sum_{\substack{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y) \\ h(x, y) = w}} \mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(divide each term; conditional PMF of } Y \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Every \(y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)\) with \(\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x) &gt; 0\) has \(h(x, y) \in \mathcal{R}(W)\), because some outcome has \(X = x\) and \(Y = y\), and \(W = h(x, y)\) there; the other values of \(y\) contribute terms equal to \(0\). So the groups \({\mathopen{}\left\{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y) : h(x, y) = w\right\}\mathclose{}}\), over \(w \in \mathcal{R}(W)\), contain every \(y\) with a non-zero term, each exactly once:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[W \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{w \in \mathcal{R}(W)} w \cdot\mathbb{P}(W = w \mid X = x)
&& \text{(conditional expectation of } W \text{)} \\
&= \sum_{w \in \mathcal{R}(W)} w \cdot\sum_{\substack{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y) \\ h(x, y) = w}} \mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(the display above)} \\
&= \sum_{w \in \mathcal{R}(W)} \sum_{\substack{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y) \\ h(x, y) = w}} w \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(distribute } w \text{ into the inner sum)} \\
&= \sum_{w \in \mathcal{R}(W)} \sum_{\substack{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y) \\ h(x, y) = w}} h(x, y) \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(} w = h(x, y) \text{ for every term of the inner sum)} \\
&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} h(x, y) \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(the groups contain every non-zero term once)} \\
&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(conditional expectation of a function of } X \text{ and } Y \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The same steps with \(\mathopen{}\left|w\right|\mathclose{}\) and \(\mathopen{}\left|h(x, y)\right|\mathclose{}\) in place of \(w\) and \(h(x, y)\) regroup a sum of non-negative terms, which is valid whether or not it converges, so one sum converges absolutely exactly when the other does, and then the regrouping above is valid too.










RemarkRemark






Remark. Definition 8 is the conditional version of LOTUS: for discrete \(X\), it is LOTUS applied under the probability measure \(\Pr(\cdot \mid X = x)\), and by Theorem 6, results about \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[W \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\) apply to it. \(Y\) can also be a pair \((Y, Z)\), with the sum or integral taken over both.










LemTheorem 7 (Linearity of conditional expectation)






Theorem 7 (Linearity of conditional expectation) For jointly distributed \(X\) and \(Y\), functions \(h_1\) and \(h_2\) whose conditional expectations given \(X = x\) are defined, and constants \(a\) and \(b\):
\[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[a h_1(X, Y) + b h_2(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}} = a{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h_1(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}} + b{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h_2(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\]
Evaluating both sides at \(X\) gives \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[a h_1(X, Y) + b h_2(X, Y) \mid X\right]\mathclose{}} = a{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h_1(X, Y) \mid X\right]\mathclose{}} + b{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h_2(X, Y) \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\).










ProofProof






Proof. For discrete \(Y\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
&{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[a h_1(X, Y) + b h_2(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}
\\&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} \mathopen{}\left(a h_1(x, y) + b h_2(x, y)\right)\mathclose{} \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(definition of conditional expectation)} \\
&= a \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} h_1(x, y) \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x) + b \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} h_2(x, y) \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(distribute the sum over the terms; factor out the constants)} \\
&= a{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h_1(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}} + b{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h_2(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(definition of conditional expectation)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Splitting the sum is valid because both sums converge absolutely. For continuous \(Y\), the same steps hold with integrals over \(y\) in place of the sums, by linearity of the integral: unlike Theorem 4, only one variable, \(y\), is integrated, against one conditional density.










ExmExample 15 (Linearity, given the first flip)






Example 15 (Linearity, given the first flip) Continuing Example 14, given \(X = 1\), \(Y\) is \(1\) or \(2\) with probability \(1/2\) each, so \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = 1\right]\mathclose{}} = 3/2\) and
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y^2 \mid X = 1\right]\mathclose{}} &= (1 + 4)/2 \\
&= 5/2.
\end{aligned}
\]
By Theorem 7 with
\[
\begin{aligned}
a &= b \\
&= 1:
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y + Y^2 \mid X = 1\right]\mathclose{}}
&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = 1\right]\mathclose{}} + {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y^2 \mid X = 1\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(linearity of conditional expectation)} \\
&= \tfrac{3}{2} + \tfrac{5}{2}
&& \text{(substitute)} \\
&= 4
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
As a check, directly:
\[
\begin{aligned}
(1 + 1) \cdot\tfrac{1}{2} + (2 + 4) \cdot\tfrac{1}{2} &= 1 + 3 \\
&= 4.
\end{aligned}
\]










LemTheorem 8 (A function of \(X\) factors out of a conditional expectation given \(X\))






Theorem 8 (A function of \(X\) factors out of a conditional expectation given \(X\)) For jointly distributed \(X\) and \(Y\), a function \(g\) of one argument, and a function \(h\) of two arguments whose conditional expectation given \(X = x\) is defined:
\[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[g(X)\,h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}} = g(x) \cdot{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\]
Evaluating both sides at \(X\) gives \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[g(X)\,h(X, Y) \mid X\right]\mathclose{}} = g(X) \cdot{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\). In particular, taking \(h = 1\) gives \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[g(X) \mid X\right]\mathclose{}} = g(X)\).










ProofProof






Proof. For discrete \(Y\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[g(X)\,h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} g(x)\,h(x, y) \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(definition of conditional expectation)} \\
&= g(x) \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} h(x, y) \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&& \text{(} g(x) \text{ does not depend on } y \text{)} \\
&= g(x) \cdot{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[h(X, Y) \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(definition of conditional expectation)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For \(h = 1\), the remaining sum is \(\sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} \mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x) = 1\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} \mathbb{P}(Y = y \mid X = x)
&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} \frac{\mathbb{P}(X = x,\, Y = y)}{\mathbb{P}(X = x)}
&& \text{(definition of the conditional PMF)} \\
&= \frac{\mathbb{P}(X = x)}{\mathbb{P}(X = x)}
&& \text{(marginal PMF from a joint PMF)} \\
&= 1
&& \text{(divide)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For continuous \(Y\), the same steps hold with integrals over \(y\) in place of the sums; the conditional density integrates to 1 by the marginal density theorem (or, in the mixed case, by the joint density-mass function’s marginal identity).










RemarkRemark






Remark. This result is often summarized as “taking out what is known” (e.g., Ross [10], Section 5.6.4): given \(X = x\), any function of \(X\) is the known constant \(g(x)\).










ExmExample 16 (Taking out the first flip)






Example 16 (Taking out the first flip) Continuing Example 15, with \(g(x) = x\) and \(h(x, y) = y\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[XY \mid X = 1\right]\mathclose{}}
&= 1 \cdot{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = 1\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(a function of } X \text{ factors out)} \\
&= \tfrac{3}{2}
&& \text{(substitute } {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = 1\right]\mathclose{}} = \tfrac{3}{2} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[XY \mid X = 0\right]\mathclose{}} &= 0 \cdot{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = 0\right]\mathclose{}} \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
\]
As a check, given \(X = 1\) the product \(XY\) equals \(Y\), which is \(1\) or \(2\) with probability \(1/2\) each.










ExrExercise 2 (Expectation of a sum, given a joint PMF)






Exercise 2 (Expectation of a sum, given a joint PMF) Let \((X, Y)\) be discrete with joint probability mass function:




\(Y = 0\)
\(Y = 1\)




\(X = 0\)
\(0.2\)
\(0.3\)


\(X = 1\)
\(0.1\)
\(0.4\)



Compute \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X + Y\right]\mathclose{}}\).










SolutionSolution






Solution. Treating \((X, Y)\) as a single discrete random object taking one of the four values \((0,0)\), \((0,1)\), \((1,0)\), \((1,1)\), LOTUS (Theorem 3) applied to \(g(x, y) = x + y\) gives directly:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X + Y\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{x \in \{0,1\}} \sum_{y \in \{0,1\}} (x + y)\,\mathbb{P}(X = x,\, Y = y) && \text{(LOTUS)} \\
&= (0{+}0)(0.2) + (0{+}1)(0.3) + (1{+}0)(0.1) + (1{+}1)(0.4) && \text{(expand the four terms)} \\
&= 0 + 0.3 + 0.1 + 0.8 && \text{(multiply)} \\
&= 1.2 && \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
As a check:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} &= 0(0.5) + 1(0.5) \\
&= 0.5
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}} &= 0(0.3) + 1(0.7) \\
&= 0.7,
\end{aligned}
\]
so
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X + Y\right]\mathclose{}} &= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} + {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}} \\
&= 1.2
\end{aligned}
\]
by linearity of expectation.







