LemTheorem 10 (Law of iterated expectations)






Theorem 10 (Law of iterated expectations) For any two random variables \(X\) and \(Y\) with \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[\mathopen{}\left|Y\right|\mathclose{}\right]\mathclose{}} &lt; \infty\):
\[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}} = {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}}\]










ProofProof






Proof. Discrete case. When \(X\) and \(Y\) are discrete, applying Definition 1 to \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}}\) and then the law of total probability applied to the countable partition \(\{X = x : x \in \mathcal{R}(X)\}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x\right]\mathclose{}} \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=x) && \text{(LOTUS for the function } x \mapsto {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x\right]\mathclose{}} \text{)}
\\&= \sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \mathopen{}\left(\sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y=y \mid X=x)\right)\mathclose{} \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=x) && \text{(definition of conditional expectation } {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x\right]\mathclose{}} \text{)}
\\&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \mathbb{P}(Y=y \mid X=x) \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=x) && \text{(exchange order of summation: Fubini, since } {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[\mathopen{}\left|Y\right|\mathclose{}\right]\mathclose{}} &lt; \infty \text{)}
\\&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y=y) && \text{(law of total probability over } X \text{)}
\\&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}} && \text{(definition of expectation of } Y \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Continuous case. When \(X\) and \(Y\) are continuous:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \int_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x\right]\mathclose{}} \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x)\, dx
&& \text{(LOTUS)}
\\&= \int_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \mathopen{}\left(\int_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(Y=y \mid X=x)\, dy\right)\mathclose{} \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x)\, dx
&& \text{(definition of conditional expectation)}
\\&= \int_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \int_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x, Y=y)\, dy\, dx
&& \text{(definition of conditional density: } \operatorname{p}(Y=y \mid X=x)\,\operatorname{p}(X=x) = \operatorname{p}(X=x, Y=y) \text{)}
\\&= \int_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\mathopen{}\left(\int_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \operatorname{p}(X=x, Y=y)\, dx\right)\mathclose{}\, dy
&& \text{(Fubini's theorem, since } {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[\mathopen{}\left|Y\right|\mathclose{}\right]\mathclose{}} &lt; \infty \text{)}
\\&= \int_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(Y=y)\, dy
&& \text{(marginal density from a joint density)}
\\&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(definition of expectation)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Mixed case, \(X\) discrete and \(Y\) continuous. With the joint density-mass function \(\operatorname{p}(X = x,\, Y = y)\), Corollary 3 applies with \(h(x, y) = y\), counting measure for \(X\), and Lebesgue measure for \(Y\); its condition (b) holds because \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[\mathopen{}\left|Y\right|\mathclose{}\right]\mathclose{}} &lt; \infty\). The sum runs over the \(x\) with \(\mathbb{P}(X = x) &gt; 0\), where Definition 6 defines \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x\right]\mathclose{}} \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=x)
&& \text{(LOTUS, discrete case)}
\\&= \sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \mathopen{}\left(\int_{\mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(Y=y \mid X=x)\,dy\right)\mathclose{} \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=x)
&& \text{(definition of conditional expectation, mixed case)}
\\&= \sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \int_{\mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(Y=y \mid X=x) \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=x)\,dy
&& \text{(move the constant } \mathbb{P}(X=x) \text{ inside the integral)}
\\&= \sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \int_{\mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x,\, Y=y)\,dy
&& \text{(definition of the conditional density, mixed case)}
\\&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(joint-distribution form of Fubini--Tonelli, with } h(x, y) = y \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Mixed case, \(X\) continuous and \(Y\) discrete. The same steps apply with the integral and the sum swapped, over the \(x\) with \(\operatorname{p}(X = x) &gt; 0\); at any other \(x\), \(\operatorname{p}(X = x,\, Y = y) = 0\) for every \(y\), because these non-negative terms sum to \(\operatorname{p}(X = x)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \int_{\mathcal{R}(X)} {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x\right]\mathclose{}} \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x)\,dx
&& \text{(LOTUS, continuous case)}
\\&= \int_{\mathcal{R}(X)} \mathopen{}\left(\sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y=y \mid X=x)\right)\mathclose{} \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x)\,dx
&& \text{(definition of conditional expectation, mixed case)}
\\&= \int_{\mathcal{R}(X)} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y=y \mid X=x) \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x)\,dx
&& \text{(move the constant } \operatorname{p}(X=x) \text{ inside the sum)}
\\&= \int_{\mathcal{R}(X)} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x,\, Y=y)\,dx
&& \text{(definition of the conditional PMF, mixed case)}
\\&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(joint-distribution form of Fubini--Tonelli, with } h(x, y) = y \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










RemarkRemark






Remark. Alternate names for this identity include: the tower rule, the tower property, the law of total expectation, and the smoothing theorem.










ExmExample 20 (Fuel economy by number of cylinders)






Example 20 (Fuel economy by number of cylinders) The Auto data record the number of engine cylinders and the fuel economy (miles per gallon) of 392 car models sold between 1970 and 1982. They come from the StatLib library at Carnegie Mellon University and ship with the book An Introduction to Statistical Learning, in the Python package ISLP (ISLP.load_data("Auto")) and the R package ISLR2 (ISLR2::Auto). This site has a copy at data/auto.csv, and both code tabs below read that file.
Choose one car at random, each with the same probability, and let \(X\) be its number of cylinders and \(Y\) its miles per gallon. Then \(\mathbb{P}(X = x)\) is the fraction of cars with \(x\) cylinders, and \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\) from Definition 5 is the average miles per gallon among those cars. The code below computes both for every \(x\), then checks Theorem 10 by averaging the conditional means with weights \(\mathbb{P}(X = x)\).

PythonR



import pandas as pd

auto = pd.read_csv("data/auto.csv")

by_cyl = auto.groupby("cylinders")["mpg"].agg(["count", "mean"])
by_cyl["P(X = x)"] = by_cyl["count"] / by_cyl["count"].sum()
by_cyl = by_cyl.rename(columns={"mean": "E(Y | X = x)"})
by_cyl = by_cyl[["count", "P(X = x)", "E(Y | X = x)"]]

lie_rhs = (by_cyl["P(X = x)"] * by_cyl["E(Y | X = x)"]).sum()
lie_lhs = auto["mpg"].mean()

by_cyl
#&gt;            count  P(X = x)  E(Y | X = x)
#&gt; cylinders                               
#&gt; 3              4  0.010204     20.550000
#&gt; 4            199  0.507653     29.283920
#&gt; 5              3  0.007653     27.366667
#&gt; 6             83  0.211735     19.973494
#&gt; 8            103  0.262755     14.963107




auto &lt;- read.csv("data/auto.csv")

counts &lt;- table(auto$cylinders)
cond_mean &lt;- tapply(auto$mpg, auto$cylinders, mean)
by_cyl &lt;- data.frame(
  count = as.vector(counts),
  "P(X = x)" = as.vector(counts) / sum(counts),
  "E(Y | X = x)" = as.vector(cond_mean),
  row.names = names(counts),
  check.names = FALSE
)

lie_rhs &lt;- sum(by_cyl[["P(X = x)"]] * by_cyl[["E(Y | X = x)"]])
lie_lhs &lt;- mean(auto$mpg)

by_cyl









The conditional mean \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X = x\right]\mathclose{}}\) is the function \(g(x)\) of Definition 7. Four-cylinder cars average 29.3 miles per gallon, and eight-cylinder cars average 15.0. Weighting the 5 conditional means by \(\mathbb{P}(X = x)\) gives 23.446, which equals the overall average miles per gallon, 23.446, as Theorem 10 states: \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}} = {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}}\).










LemTheorem 11 (Conditional law of iterated expectations)






Theorem 11 (Conditional law of iterated expectations) For random variables \(X\), \(Y\), and \(Z\) with \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[\mathopen{}\left|Y\right|\mathclose{}\right]\mathclose{}} &lt; \infty\):
\[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid Z\right]\mathclose{}} = {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X,Z\right]\mathclose{}} \mid Z\right]\mathclose{}}\]










ProofProof






Proof. Choose a value \(z\) with positive probability (discrete case) or positive density (continuous case). Conditioning every probability on \(Z = z\) gives a probability measure, and the proof of Theorem 10 goes through under it, line by line.
Discrete case.
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X,Z\right]\mathclose{}} \mid Z=z\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x,Z=z\right]\mathclose{}} \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=x \mid Z=z) && \text{(expectation given } Z=z \text{)}
\\&= \sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \mathopen{}\left(\sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y=y \mid X=x,Z=z)\right)\mathclose{} \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=x \mid Z=z) && \text{(definition of } {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x,Z=z\right]\mathclose{}} \text{)}
\\&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\sum_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \mathbb{P}(Y=y \mid X=x,Z=z) \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=x \mid Z=z) && \text{(exchange order of summation)}
\\&= \sum_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\mathbb{P}(Y=y \mid Z=z) && \text{(law of total probability given } Z=z \text{)}
\\&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid Z=z\right]\mathclose{}} && \text{(definition of conditional expectation given } Z=z \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Continuous case.
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X,Z\right]\mathclose{}} \mid Z=z\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \int_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x,Z=z\right]\mathclose{}} \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x \mid Z=z)\, dx
&& \text{(expectation given } Z=z \text{)}
\\&= \int_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \mathopen{}\left(\int_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(Y=y \mid X=x,Z=z)\, dy\right)\mathclose{} \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x \mid Z=z)\, dx
&& \text{(definition of } {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=x,Z=z\right]\mathclose{}} \text{)}
\\&= \int_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \int_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(X=x, Y=y \mid Z=z)\, dy\, dx
&& \text{(product of conditional densities is the joint conditional density)}
\\&= \int_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\mathopen{}\left(\int_{x \in \mathcal{R}(X)} \operatorname{p}(X=x, Y=y \mid Z=z)\, dx\right)\mathclose{}\, dy
&& \text{(Fubini's theorem)}
\\&= \int_{y \in \mathcal{R}(Y)} y \cdot\operatorname{p}(Y=y \mid Z=z)\, dy
&& \text{(marginalize over } x \text{)}
\\&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid Z=z\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(definition of conditional expectation given } Z=z \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Since the two sides agree at every such \(z\), they agree as random variables (functions of \(Z\)): \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid Z\right]\mathclose{}} = {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X,Z\right]\mathclose{}} \mid Z\right]\mathclose{}}\).










RemarkRemark






Remark. This identity is the tower rule applied conditionally on \(Z\).










ExmExample 21 (Marginal expectation from conditional expectations)






Example 21 (Marginal expectation from conditional expectations) Suppose \(X\) is a binary random variable indicating treatment assignment (\(X=1\) treated, \(X=0\) control), with \(\mathbb{P}(X=1) = 0.5\), and suppose the outcome \(Y\) has conditional expectations:
\[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=1\right]\mathclose{}} = 10, \quad {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=0\right]\mathclose{}} = 6\]
By the law of iterated expectations (Theorem 10):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}}
&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(law of iterated expectations)}
\\&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=1\right]\mathclose{}} \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=1) + {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y \mid X=0\right]\mathclose{}} \cdot\mathbb{P}(X=0)
&& \text{(LOTUS over the two values of } X \text{)}
\\&= 10 \cdot 0.5 + 6 \cdot 0.5
&& \text{(substitute)}
\\&= 5 + 3
&& \text{(multiply)}
\\&= 8
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExrExercise 3 (Both-discrete case, infinite support: joint PMF)






Exercise 3 (Both-discrete case, infinite support: joint PMF) Let \(X\) and \(Y\) be independent, each Geometric on
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{R}(X) &= \mathcal{R}(Y) \\
&= \{0, 1, 2, \dots\}
\end{aligned}
\]
with \(\mathbb{P}(X = x) = (1-p)\,p^x\) for a fixed \(p \in (0, 1)\) (\(X\) counts the number of failures before the first success in a sequence of independent trials with success probability \(1-p\); likewise for \(Y\)). Unlike Exercise 2, the support here is countably infinite. The joint PMF is \(\mathbb{P}(X = x,\, Y = y) = (1-p)^2\,p^{x+y}\).
Compute \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X + Y\right]\mathclose{}}\).










SolutionSolution






Solution. Compute \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X + Y\right]\mathclose{}}\) using Corollary 3 with
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mu_X &= \mu_Y \\
&= \text{counting measure}
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(h(x, y) = x + y\). Since \(h(x,y) = x + y \ge 0\) for every \((x,y)\) in this support, condition (a) holds, so Corollary 3 (via Tonelli’s theorem) guarantees the order of this now-infinite double sum is exchangeable — unlike the finite case, elementary algebra alone could not establish this.
The derivation uses the standard geometric-series facts \(\sum_{y=0}^{\infty} p^y = \frac{1}{1-p}\) and \(\sum_{y=0}^{\infty} y\,p^y = \frac{p}{(1-p)^2}\) (e.g. Casella and Berger [6]). By Corollary 3 (both-discrete case), summing over \(y\) first for each fixed \(x\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X + Y\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} (x + y)\,\mathbb{P}(X = x,\, Y = y)
&& \text{(joint-distribution form of Fubini--Tonelli)}
\\&= \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} (x + y)(1-p)^2 p^{x+y}
&& \text{(substitute the joint PMF)}
\\&= \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} (1-p)^2 p^x \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} (x + y)\,p^y
&& \text{(factor } (1-p)^2 p^x \text{ out of the inner sum)}
\\&= \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} (1-p)^2 p^x
  \mathopen{}\left(x \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} p^y + \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} y\,p^y\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(split the inner sum; factor out } x \text{)}
\\&= \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} (1-p)^2 p^x
  \mathopen{}\left(\frac{x}{1-p} + \frac{p}{(1-p)^2}\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(geometric-series facts)}
\\&= \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} p^x \mathopen{}\left[x(1-p) + p\right]\mathclose{}
&& \text{(multiply } (1-p)^2 \text{ into the parentheses)}
\\&= (1-p) \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} x\,p^x + p \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} p^x
&& \text{(distribute the sum over the terms; factor out the constants)}
\\&= (1-p) \cdot\frac{p}{(1-p)^2} + p \cdot\frac{1}{1-p}
&& \text{(geometric-series facts)}
\\&= \frac{p}{1-p} + \frac{p}{1-p}
&& \text{(cancel } 1 - p \text{)}
\\&= \frac{2p}{1-p}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
As a check,
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} &= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}} \\
&= \frac{p}{1-p}
\end{aligned}
\]
(the mean of this Geometric distribution; Casella and Berger [6]), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X + Y\right]\mathclose{}} &= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} + {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}} \\
&= \frac{2p}{1-p}
\end{aligned}
\]
by linearity of expectation, matching.


Show code
