RemarkRemark






Remark. \(Y\) takes only finitely many values here (two), so \(\sum_{y \in \{0,1\}} \int_0^{y+1} x\,f_{X,Y}(x,\,y)\,dx\) is just linearity of the integral applied to a two-term sum — \(\int g + \int k = \int (g + k)\) — not a genuine interchange of summation and integration order. If \(Y\) had a countably infinite range instead, the sum of integrals would be an infinite series, and Corollary 3’s guarantee would be load-bearing.










ExrExercise 5 (Mixed case, infinite discrete support)






Exercise 5 (Mixed case, infinite discrete support) Let \(Y\) be Geometric on \(\{0, 1, 2, \dots\}\) with \(\mathbb{P}(Y = y) = (1-q)\,q^y\) for a fixed \(q \in (0, 1)\) and, given \(Y = y\), let \(X \mid Y = y \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(0,\, y+1)\). Unlike Exercise 4, \(Y\)’s range here is countably infinite.
Compute \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}}\).










SolutionSolution






Solution. Compute \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}}\) using Corollary 3 with \(\mu_X = \text{Lebesgue measure}\), \(\mu_Y = \text{counting measure}\), and \(h(x, y) = x\).
The joint density w.r.t. Lebesgue \(\times\) counting measure is
\[
\begin{aligned}
f_{X,Y}(x, y) &= f_{X \mid Y}(x \mid y)\,\mathbb{P}(Y = y) \\
&= \frac{(1-q)\,q^y}{y+1}
\end{aligned}
\]
for \(x \in [0, y+1]\).
Since \(h(x, y) = x \ge 0\) on this support, condition (a) holds, so Corollary 3 (via Tonelli’s theorem) guarantees the now-infinite sum-of-integrals expression is valid — unlike in Exercise 4, this Fubini–Tonelli justification is required because the sum is infinite rather than finite.
By Corollary 3 (mixed case):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}}
&= \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} \int_0^{y+1} x\,f_{X,Y}(x,\,y)\,dx
&& \text{(joint-distribution form of Fubini--Tonelli, mixed case)}
\\&= \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} \frac{(1-q)\,q^y}{y+1} \int_0^{y+1} x\,dx
&& \text{(} f_{X,Y}(x, y) \text{ is constant in } x \text{ on } [0, y+1] \text{)}
\\&= \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} \frac{(1-q)\,q^y}{y+1} \cdot\frac{(y+1)^2}{2}
&& \text{(integrate)}
\\&= \frac{1-q}{2} \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} (y+1)\,q^y
&& \text{(cancel } y + 1 \text{; factor out } \tfrac{1-q}{2} \text{)}
\\&= \frac{1-q}{2} \mathopen{}\left(\sum_{y=0}^{\infty} y\,q^y + \sum_{y=0}^{\infty} q^y\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(distribute the sum over the terms)}
\\&= \frac{1-q}{2} \mathopen{}\left(\frac{q}{(1-q)^2} + \frac{1}{1-q}\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(geometric-series facts)}
\\&= \frac{1-q}{2} \cdot\frac{q + (1-q)}{(1-q)^2}
&& \text{(common denominator)}
\\&= \frac{1-q}{2} \cdot\frac{1}{(1-q)^2}
&& \text{(simplify the numerator)}
\\&= \frac{1}{2(1-q)}
&& \text{(cancel } 1 - q \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
using the same geometric-series facts as Exercise 3 (e.g. Casella and Berger [6]).
As a check using the law of iterated expectations (Theorem 10) and the expectation of a linear function (Corollary 1): \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X \mid Y=y\right]\mathclose{}} = \frac{y+1}{2}\) (the mean of \(\mathrm{Uniform}(0,y+1)\)) and \({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}} = \frac{q}{1-q}\) (the mean of this Geometric distribution; Casella and Berger [6]), so:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}} &= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X \mid Y\right]\mathclose{}}\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(law of iterated expectations)}
\\&= {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[\frac{Y+1}{2}\right]\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(} {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X \mid Y = y\right]\mathclose{}} = \tfrac{y+1}{2} \text{)}
\\&= \frac{{\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}} + 1}{2}
&& \text{(expectation of a linear function of one random variable)}
\\&= \frac{1}{2}\mathopen{}\left(\frac{q}{1-q} + 1\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(substitute } {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[Y\right]\mathclose{}} \text{)}
\\&= \frac{1}{2} \cdot\frac{q + (1-q)}{1-q}
&& \text{(common denominator)}
\\&= \frac{1}{2(1-q)}
&& \text{(simplify the numerator)}
\end{aligned}
\]
matching.


Show code
q &lt;- 0.5
exact_ex &lt;- 1 / (2 * (1 - q))

n_trunc &lt;- 2000
y_vals &lt;- 0:n_trunc
py_vals &lt;- (1 - q) * q^y_vals
cond_mean_x &lt;- (y_vals + 1) / 2
trunc_ex &lt;- sum(cond_mean_x * py_vals)

tibble::tibble(
  quantity = c("exact (closed form)", "truncated sum-of-integrals"),
  value = round(c(exact_ex, trunc_ex), 6)
) |&gt;
  pander::pander()









